<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huaca de la Luna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 100–<a href="/years/800">800 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Temple of the Moon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huaca de la Luna, 'Temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a>', is a great adobe temple at the Moche capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a>, near Trujillo on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Rebuilt in successive layers between about 100 and 800 CE, it served as a religious centre where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> took place. Its walls preserve brightly painted reliefs of the Moche deity often called the Decapitator and other figures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huaca-de-la-luna.jpg" alt="Huaca de la Luna" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joel Takv · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huaca_de_la_Luna_jt02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
